# Copy to .dev.vars (gitignored, never commit the real values) for local
# `wrangler dev`. In production these are set with `wrangler secret put
# <NAME>` from this directory - wrangler.jsonc carries no `vars`/`secrets`
# block on purpose, because a secret typed into a committed config in a
# PUBLIC repo is the exact failure this file exists to avoid.
#
# Both .dev.vars and .dev.vars.example are wrangler's own convention (the
# Workers analogue of .env / .env.example), distinct from Next.js's .env
# files in the catalogue project - this Worker reads NEITHER of those.

# Postgres, POOLED connection (hostname contains "-pooler"). This Worker only
# ever runs short application queries, never `prisma migrate`, so it needs
# only the pooled string - DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED (migrations only) stays a
# local-machine concern for prisma/schema.prisma in this project and is never
# a Worker secret.
#
# DO NOT TYPE THIS BY HAND. Run, from this directory:
#   neon link --project-id holy-sunset-91521586 --branch production -y
# which writes it (and DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED, for local `prisma migrate`) into
# .env in this directory - copy the pooled value across into .dev.vars by
# hand once, since wrangler and the Prisma CLI read two different files here.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@ep-xxxx-pooler.REGION.aws.neon.tech/neondb?sslmode=require

# Hostinger's HTTP Email API, for sending the magic link from
# contact@counterscent.com (mailbox already live - MX/DKIM/SPF confirmed in
# departments/web-development/counterscent-dns-migration.md). This replaced a
# hand-written SMTP client on 2026-09-16; src/lib/mailer.ts explains why, and
# why that client was deleted rather than kept as a fallback.
#
# THE TOKEN MUST BE ISSUED SEPARATELY FOR THIS WORKER. Do not paste in the
# value of the machine-level HOSTINGER_MAIL_TOKEN that the founder's own
# hostinger-email MCP server uses. They reach the same mailbox and neither is
# narrower - the point is that revoking this one on suspicion must not break
# the founder's own tooling, because a revocation with a cost attached is one
# that gets put off. Same rule the root CLAUDE.md records for keeping
# HOSTINGER_MAIL_TOKEN off the shared HOSTINGER_API_TOKEN.
#
# Be clear-eyed about what this grants: send, and also read, search, delete
# (including whole-folder) and webhook management on that mailbox. Hostinger's
# Email API publishes no send-only scope - that was checked against its full
# operation list, not assumed. If this value ever escapes, revoke it AND
# enumerate the mailbox's webhooks, because revocation alone does not remove a
# webhook created with it.
HOSTINGER_MAIL_API_TOKEN=

# Which mailbox the token above sends from. Discover it with
# `GET /api/v1/me`, whose data.mailboxes[] is the only way to list them (there
# is no standalone list-mailboxes endpoint). The value below is the real,
# non-secret resource id for contact@counterscent.com - an identifier, not a
# credential, and useless without the token.
HOSTINGER_MAILBOX_ID=AC9278a32f3d4ca8bd1119f31c2d1c

# NEVER COMMIT THE REAL VALUES. This repo is public. .dev.vars is gitignored
# at the repo root; verify with `git check-ignore .dev.vars` before assuming
# it, the same rule the root CLAUDE.md states for any downloaded/licensed
# data landing somewhere unexpected.
#
# All of the above are read with a presence check at request time
# (src/lib/env.ts) - a missing secret degrades the affected page to
# `notShipped()` rather than failing silently or lying about success. Setting
# none of them is a valid, expected state, not a bug: this Worker was built
# with the mail token possibly still unset (see SUBSCRIPTION-PROGRESS.md, the
# step 5 entry, for whether it was set at the time).
